opam-version: "2.0"
maintainer: "Boris D. <borisd@gmx.com>"
substs: ["ios-arm32.conf"]
patches: ["patches/ios-arm32.patch"]
build: [
  ["/bin/sh" "./build.sh" "%{prefix}%" "%{conf-ios-arm32:cc}%"]
]
install: [
  ["/bin/sh" "./install.sh" "%{prefix}%"]
]
remove: [
  ["/bin/sh" "./remove.sh" "%{prefix}%"]
]
depends: [
  # The cross compiler is itself bytecode and runs on the switch's ocamlrun,
  # so the host compiler must share 4.14's bytecode and .cmi magic.
  "ocaml" {>= "4.14.0" & < "4.15.0"}
  "ocamlfind" {>= "1.5.4"}
  "conf-ios-arm32"
]
conflicts: ["ocaml-ios32" "ocaml-ios64"]
available: os = "macos"
synopsis: "OCaml bytecode cross-compiler for 32-bit ARM iOS devices"
description: """
Builds OCaml 4.14.4 -- the last OCaml 4 release -- as a bytecode
cross-compiler for the 32-bit ARM iOS target selected by conf-ios-arm32,
which is where the architecture, SDK and deployment target are chosen.
Includes unix, str and threads. Verified on an iPod touch 2G (armv6)
running iPhone OS 4.2.1.

Bytecode only. OCaml's 32-bit ARM *native* backend needed continual upkeep
for Mach-O assembly output, which is why native 32-bit iOS support stalled
at 4.04; none of that code is involved in a bytecode build, so this tracks
a current release instead.

Only the runtime and the C stubs are architecture-specific: a bytecode-only
compiler reports architecture "none", so the compiler, the stdlib and the
.cmi files would be identical whichever SUBARCH conf-ios-arm32 names.

Two runtimes are installed. ios-sysroot/bin/ocamlrun is a host binary and
runs the cross compiler itself; ios-sysroot/device/ocamlrun is the target
one, to be copied to /usr/local/bin on the device, where bytecode
executables produced by this compiler expect it. Because the build has no
shared libraries, linking anything with C stubs selects -custom, producing
a self-contained binary that needs nothing installed on the device.

Do not sign -custom executables: ldid rebuilds the Mach-O from its load
commands and discards the bytecode appended past the last segment.
"""
